COMMIT e8bc7870d2f916920ee9e6392645caab4da5db12
fix(bench): FIX-134 put warmedUp in the bench record

`warmedUp` existed only as a local at NodeBenchMain.java:314. It decides how
long the warm-up's settle waits at :315, but it never reached the record, so no
test could see it and an understated value passed in silence. One line now puts
it beside the `warmupFailed` that was already there. :314, the settle at :315 and
Harness are untouched, and bench/RESULTS.md is not touched at all, so this commit
claims no benchmark number.

Red observed before the code: `expected: <2> but was: <null>` at the new
assertion. Green after: the filtered case 12/0/0/0 and the whole module 27/0/0/0,
both BUILD SUCCESS. Every run here carries `-Djacoco.skip=true`, which a filtered
`-Pit` run requires, so NONE of it is evidence about coverage and none is claimed;
that comes from the next full unfiltered reactor run.

Four hand mutants, each killed by the assertion at :218 in about 28 seconds rather
than by a timeout. Two were mine: `.put("warmedUp", 0)` dying as `<0>`, and the
`.put` removed dying as `<null>`. The reviewer named two better ones, and one of
them corrected me:

- M3 overstates the RECORD only, `.put("warmedUp", o.warmup())`, leaving the
  computation at :314 right. The settle therefore still works, the run reaches the
  assertion, and `<5>` dies there. This is the only way to see a 5 caught by an
  assertion rather than a timeout.
- M4 is an off-by-one at :314, `o.warmup() - warmupFailed - 1`, dying as `<1>`.
  That is the true surviving class and my own pair tested it not at all.

`cmp` proved NodeBenchMain.java byte-identical after every mutant; restores used
`cp` plus `touch`, never `mv`.

The one review FAIL was documentation, and the path it took is the reason it is
written down here rather than quietly fixed. I claimed in the packet that the
overstated mutant also dies by assertion. It does not: `warmedUp` drives the
settle that runs BEFORE the record is written, and that settle waits for five
`messages` rows when only two exist, because the three refused saves are rolled
back; its deadline is DRAIN at 10 minutes against the class @Timeout of 300 s, so
the timeout fires first and the assertion is never reached. The test-author took
that claim from my packet and wrote it into a code comment. It was also in the
BACKLOG row. I corrected the row but not the packet, so it survived in the packet
and had already re-entered the test. Then my correction was itself imprecise,
because M3 shows an overstated record IS caught — the truth depends on whether the
5 comes from the computation or only from the record. One sentence, four places,
two review rounds. The packet keeps the wrong bullet struck rather than deleted,
so the next reader sees what was claimed and what disproved it.

Also in this commit, because they share a file rather than a task: the FIX-133 row
corrections made earlier today, which remove a demand for a FlowWorker lease
assertion that FlowWorkerTest:90 and :1030 already make, and record that the
`policy.records(..., FAILED)` guard is not killable. agent/NOTES.md is deliberately
NOT in this commit; its new entries belong to FIX-133 and go with it.

